Abby’s camera fan whirred beside them. Air moved through the supply duct.

Diane watched Evan’s thumb hover above the switch. No segment flickered. Beyond his hand, the service opening stayed black beneath the pipes.

“Keep it exactly where it is,” she said.

## Scene 3 — Two Devices

“Keep it exactly where it is,” Diane said.

Evan held the meter at arm’s length. Its dark display faced Abby’s camera. The device remained just beyond the crawlspace ledge, beneath the lowest pipe, with Evan’s wrist braced against concrete.

Abby moved closer until the meter, Evan’s hand, and the service opening filled her frame. “Power loss at four-twelve and thirty-eight seconds. No visible impact. Operator pressed the switch twice after failure.”

“Three times,” Evan said.

“The third press was after Diane told you to stop.”

Diane crouched beside him. “Take your thumb off the switch.”

He lifted it.

“Hold position.”

The meter’s plastic case reflected Abby’s camera light. Dust clung to the edge nearest the crawlspace. The battery cover sat flush. No wire, pipe, or metal fitting touched the device.

Miriam stood behind Diane, one bare hand cupped against her jaw. Her assistant had backed toward the shelving with the folded blanket held against her chest.

Evan said, “It worked until I moved it toward them.”

Diane pointed toward Abby without looking away from the meter. “Record that as his interpretation.”

Abby repeated the wording and time.

“I’m describing what happened,” Evan said.

“You moved it from your chest to the ledge. The device lost power during the move. Those are the observations.”

“And the place she identified is where it stopped.”

“Miriam identified the place after you described the old investigation and brought everyone to the opening.”

Miriam lowered her hand. “Diane.”

Miriam spoke without raising her voice. Diane stood.

“I directed attention there,” Miriam said. “Evan supplied information. Both statements belong in the record.”

Evan turned his head. His arm remained extended. “You still sense them?”

“Yes.”

“Right now?”

“Yes.”

Diane watched Miriam’s face. Her eyes stayed on the opening, but the muscles along her mouth had tightened. She pressed her tongue once against the inside of her cheek.

“What changed?” Diane asked.

“The pressure moved forward.”

“Toward the meter?”

“Toward us.”

The assistant shifted another half step from the shelving. Her boot scraped concrete.

Diane said, “We are leaving this room before we open the device. Evan, withdraw in one motion. Keep the display toward Abby.”

He drew his arm back. The meter passed beneath the pipe, across the ledge, and into the room. The screen stayed black. Abby tracked it without losing the battery cover or Evan’s hand.

At Diane’s direction, they returned through the fire door and entered the equipment alcove across the corridor. The alcove had once housed pump controls for a removed boiler loop. A steel workbench ran along one wall beneath numbered disconnect boxes. The district had capped the old lines, painted the handles red, and stenciled OUT OF SERVICE across each cabinet. A fluorescent strip above the bench produced a steady electrical hum.

Diane placed a clean shop towel on the bench.

“Meter in the center,” she said.

Evan set it down.

“Hands away.”

He stepped back. Abby filmed the meter from above, then the underside, battery cover, switch, and serial label. Diane opened her facilities pouch and removed two sealed alkaline batteries with the purchase date written across the package in black marker.

Evan pointed his camera toward the sealed package. “You carry batteries now?”

“I carry batteries when equipment failure may become evidence.”

Abby logged the installed cells as an unmatched pair from Evan’s case, installed that afternoon, with no retained packaging or prior voltage test. One carried a shallow dent near its negative terminal.

Diane pulled nitrile gloves from the wall dispenser and put them on. She removed the battery cover while Abby recorded the movement. The two installed cells matched in brand, but one had a shallow dent near the negative terminal. Neither carried a date label.

“Was that dent present when you installed it?” Diane asked.

Evan leaned toward the bench. “I have no idea.”

Diane removed both cells and placed them beside the meter without letting them roll. A faint gray smear marked one contact inside the compartment. The opposite spring sat slightly off center.

“Corrosion?” Evan asked.

“Surface residue.” Diane angled the compartment toward Abby. “No conclusion until it is cleaned and tested.”

Miriam entered the alcove last. She stopped beneath the fluorescent strip and rubbed her bare fingertips together.

“Cold?” Diane asked.

“No.”

“Pins and needles?”

“Something like static, except it starts at the elbow.”

“Which arm?”

“The right.”

“Since when?”

“The crawlspace.”

Diane glanced at the disconnect boxes. “You are standing below live electrical service.”

“The sensation began before I came in here.”

“I’m recording the environment.”

“I know.”

Miriam’s assistant offered her glove. Miriam left it in the assistant’s hand.

Diane cleaned the battery contacts with a dry swab, inserted the labeled pair, replaced the cover, and slid the meter toward Evan.

“Power it on with one press.”

He pressed the button. The green indicator lit. The display showed all segments, cleared, and settled at zero.

Evan began turning his camera toward Miriam.

“Keep the camera on the device,” Diane said.

He obeyed.

Abby stated the replacement time and battery source. Diane marked the bench position with two short strips of blue tape. The meter remained on for sixty seconds. Its reading fluctuated between zero and one-tenth.

Evan asked to repeat the crawlspace route. Diane required a stationary comparison first. Evan accused her of narrowing the question; his camera battery icon turned red while he spoke.

Miriam’s assistant offered a compact flashlight. Diane filmed its scratched cap and cold aluminum body. The assistant reported fresh batteries but retained no package and had used the light outside during unloading. One press produced a steady beam.

“Working at four-sixteen,” Abby said.

Diane switched it off and set it parallel to the meter. “Both devices remain on the bench for two minutes. No handling.”

Miriam moved closer to the doorway. She looked down the corridor toward the crawlspace room.

The fluorescent strip hummed. Water crossed overhead through a pair of copper lines. Farther away, a cart rolled across a floor joint and stopped.

Evan replaced his camera battery.

Miriam touched her right forearm with her left hand. “This is separate.”

Diane said, “Separate from what?”

“The ones in the low space.”

Evan raised the camera before the new battery door had fully latched. “What is separate?”

Miriam looked into the corridor. “One presence. Closer to the rooms people use.”

“What kind?”

“I have no category for it.”

“Human?”

“I cannot tell.”

“Dangerous?”

Miriam’s hand tightened around her forearm. “It wants a reaction.”

Evan stepped into the doorway. “From us?”

“From whoever notices it.”

Diane checked the timer on her recorder. Forty-three seconds remained in the stationary test.

Evan said, “So it feeds on fear too.”

“I would separate the two impressions.”

“Why?”

“The crawlspace felt crowded and avoidant. This feels singular. The pressure increases with attention and recedes when attention shifts.”

“A taunter.”

“That is a useful word for the behavior. It is not a name.”

Evan repeated the word toward his camera. “A taunting presence has moved out of the crawlspace and into the corridor.”

Diane pressed the marker button. “Miriam described a separate impression. No movement has been observed.”

Evan swung the camera toward her. “You heard what she said.”

“I also heard what you added.”

The timer reached two minutes. The meter and flashlight remained powered off on the towel, exactly where Diane had placed them.

She pointed to the meter. “Evan, one press.”

The green indicator lit again.

“Assistant, flashlight.”

The assistant pressed the switch. The beam struck the workbench, bright and stable.

“Both functional after stationary hold,” Abby said.

Diane marked the result. “Next test: meter only. Same route, no speech during transit.”

Evan picked it up.

A hard metallic strike sounded from the corridor.

The assistant flinched and turned. Evan’s camera rose toward the doorway. Miriam released her forearm.

The sound had carried a brief, bright ring, closer to a key hitting pipe than to the dull contraction they had heard in the crawlspace. Diane stepped into the corridor and looked left, then right. The crawlspace door remained latched open against its wall stop. The classroom-storage door farther down stood closed. No person occupied the intervening hall.

“Where?” Evan asked.

Diane raised one hand for quiet.

The ring thinned along the pipe chase above them. A smaller vibration reached the steel cabinet behind Diane a moment later, making the sound seem to pass over their position. The structure offered no single point of origin.

Abby came to the doorway without crossing it. Her camera remained trained down the corridor.

Miriam said, “It was to the right.”

“The pipe chase runs right,” Diane said.

“What struck it?”

“I have no visual source.”

Evan stepped past her. “It sounded like keys.”

Diane checked both his hands. The meter occupied his right. His left held the camera grip. Abby’s keys were clipped inside her closed coat pocket. Miriam and her assistant carried no visible metal.

Diane checked her own ring. The school keys hung beneath her coat, secured to a retractable line at her belt. She drew them out and held them in Abby’s frame. The cluster had stayed inside the coat.

“Mine remained secured,” she said.

Evan turned his camera toward the key ring. “The same sound came through the basement during the first case.”

“Different corridor. Different time. One strike.”

“You know it sounded the same.”

“It sounded metallic.”

Miriam stood behind them, her face angled toward the classroom-storage door. “The closer presence seems to react to comparison.”

Diane looked at her. “What produced that interpretation?”

“The sensation increased when Evan mentioned the first case.”

“Where?”

Miriam touched the inside of her right elbow. “Here. Then across the palm.”

Evan extended the meter toward her. “Read this.”

“Keep it away from her body,” Diane said.

He stopped two feet short. The green light remained on. The display moved to two-tenths, then returned to zero.

“Small spike,” he said.

“Within the device’s ordinary movement so far.”

Miriam held her palm toward the meter without touching it. Her fingers trembled once and steadied.

“Does it respond to you?” Evan asked.

“I feel the same pressure.”

“Ask it to affect the meter.”

“No.”

His camera lowered slightly. “Why not?”

“Because you are already treating every reaction as a reason to continue.”

No one answered her.

Diane saw the assistant look from Miriam to the dark classroom-storage door. The blanket had slipped lower against her chest. She pulled it up again.

“Resume the route test,” Diane said. “No questions. No invitations. We return to the crawlspace threshold, hold for ten seconds, and come back.”

Evan nodded, but he kept the camera aimed at Miriam as they moved. Diane took the lead. Abby followed behind Evan, filming the meter and his hand. Miriam and her assistant remained at the alcove doorway.

They crossed the first floor joint. The display read zero.

At the fire door, one-tenth.

Inside the crawlspace room, zero again.

Diane stopped at the blue tape mark Abby had placed near the ledge during the earlier baseline. “Arm forward. Same height.”

Evan extended the meter. The green indicator stayed on.

“Ten seconds,” Diane said.

Abby counted.

At four, the flashlight beam from the equipment alcove swept across the corridor wall behind them.

